The answer depends on the framework. The context covers three.

## Next.js

Add a `loading.js` file next to your `page.js`. Next.js automatically wraps the page in a `<Suspense>` boundary and uses your loading component as the fallback [1]. Return a spinner from it instead of a skeleton:

```tsx
// app/feed/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  // Or a custom loading skeleton component
  return <p>Loading...</p>
}
```
[2]

The loading UI appears instantly from the server while the route segment streams in, and the real content replaces it when it's ready [1][2]. Keep the UI light-weight [2]. The docs recommend loading states that are meaningful, such as skeletons, spinners, or a small part of the future screen like a cover photo or title [3].

The docs don't provide a built-in spinner component for Next.js, so you'd supply your own markup or component.

## Angular

Resolvers block navigation while they run. To show feedback, read the router's current navigation state and render an indicator:

```angular-ts
import {Component, computed, inject} from '@angular/core';
import {Router, RouterOutlet} from '@angular/router';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [RouterOutlet],
  template: `
    @if (isNavigating()) {
      <div class="loading-bar">Loading...</div>
    }
    <router-outlet />
  `,
})
export class App {
  private router = inject(Router);
  isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation());
}
```
[4]

Alternatively, use non-blocking route resources to render components immediately with skeleton or loading indicators [4].

## React Native

The docs provided only say that `ActivityIndicator` displays a circular loading indicator [5]. They don't cover how to wire it to page data loading.

There are two options in the docs.

## 1. `useSearchParams` hook (any Client Component)

`useSearchParams` is a Client Component hook that returns a **read-only** version of `URLSearchParams`. [1]

```tsx
'use client'

import { useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation'

export default function SearchBar() {
  const searchParams = useSearchParams()

  const search = searchParams.get('search')

  // URL -> `/dashboard?search=my-project`
  // `search` -> 'my-project'
  return <>Search: {search}</>
}
```
[1]

## 2. `searchParams` prop with `use` (Client Component pages)

If the Client Component is a **page**, you can read the `searchParams` prop. It's a promise, and since Client Components can't be `async`, unwrap it with React's `use` function. [2][4]

```tsx
'use client'
import { use } from 'react'

export default function Page({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const filters = use(searchParams).filters
}
```
[4]

Things to note about the prop approach:
- `searchParams` is a plain JavaScript object, not a `URLSearchParams` instance. Repeated keys become arrays (e.g. `/shop?a=1&a=2` gives `{ a: ['1', '2'] }`). [4]
- Using it opts the page into dynamic rendering at request time. [4]
- In Next.js 14 and earlier it was a synchronous prop. Synchronous access still works in Next.js 15 for backwards compatibility, but will be deprecated. [4]

Call `redirect('/login')` from `next/navigation` inside the Server Component when the user isn't authenticated. It throws a `NEXT_REDIRECT` error and stops rendering the route segment [1].

```tsx
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'

async function fetchTeam(id: string) {
  const res = await fetch('https://...')
  if (!res.ok) return undefined
  return res.json()
}

export default async function Profile({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params
  const team = await fetchTeam(id)

  if (!team) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  // ...
}
```
[1]

You don't need `return redirect()`, because `redirect` uses the TypeScript `never` type [1].

**Alternatives from the docs:**

- **Proxy:** to redirect based on a condition such as authentication before rendering, use `NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))` in `proxy.ts`. A `matcher` limits it to specific routes, such as `/dashboard/:path*` [2].
- **Login UI instead of a redirect:** call `unauthorized()` and render a login UI from `app/unauthorized.tsx`. This shows the login UI rather than redirecting to `/login` [3][4][5].

The example in [1] checks for a missing `team` rather than a session. The docs provided don't show a session check with `redirect()` specifically, but the same pattern applies with your own auth check in place of the `team` condition.

Wrap a folder name in square brackets to create a dynamic segment. For a blog, that means `app/blog/[slug]/page.js`, where `[slug]` is the dynamic segment [2]. `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` matches URLs like `/blog/my-first-post` [5].

## Reading the slug

Dynamic segments are passed to your page as the `params` prop. It's a Promise, so `await` it and destructure `slug`:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>
}
```
[1]

For example, `/blog/a` gives `params` of `{ slug: 'a' }` [2].

## Fetching content by slug

You can use the slug to load the specific content for the page:

```tsx
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[3]

`params` is also passed to `layout`, `route`, and `generateMetadata` functions [2].

Yes. Return the full list of paths from `generateStaticParams` in your dynamic route, and Next.js will prerender every one of them at build time [1]:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
[1]

Notes:

- `generateStaticParams` statically generates routes at build time instead of on-demand at request time [5].
- Calls to `fetch` inside `generateStaticParams` are automatically deduplicated, which avoids repeated network calls for the same data and speeds up build time [5].
- You must always return an array, even if it's empty. Otherwise the route is rendered dynamically [1].
- If you only want some posts prerendered, return a partial list. The rest are generated the first time they're visited, or you can set `dynamicParams = false` to make them 404 [1].
- Prerendering every page increases build work and produces output that has to be stored and deployed, and many routes may never be visited before your next deployment [3].

Export a `Metadata` object from each route's `page.js` (or `layout.js`) with its own `title` and `description`. [3]

```tsx
// app/blog/page.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: '...',
}

export default function Page() {}
```

[2][3]

The `description` field renders as a `<meta name="description" ...>` tag in the `<head>`. [4]

```html
<meta name="description" content="The React Framework for the Web" />
```

[4]

## Sharing a title pattern across pages

If you want a common format for all titles, define a `title.template` in a parent `layout.js`, with a required `title.default`. Child pages then only set a short `title`:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: '%s | Acme',
    default: 'Acme',
  },
}
```

```tsx
// app/about/page.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'About',
}
// Output: <title>About | Acme</title>
```

[1]

Notes:
- A template doesn't apply to a `title` in the same segment's `page.js`, only to child segments. [1]
- A page that doesn't define a title falls back to the closest parent's resolved title. [5]
- To skip the parent template for a page, use `title.absolute`. [5]

```tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: { absolute: 'About' },
}
// Output: <title>About</title>
```

[5]

The docs provided cover only static metadata via the `metadata` object. They don't show how to generate per-page metadata dynamically with the `generateMetadata` function.

Call the async `cookies()` function from `next/headers`, `await` it, and use `.get()` on the result:

```tsx
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  const theme = cookieStore.get('theme')
  return '...'
}
```
[1][2]

To check whether a cookie exists without reading it, use `.has()`:

```tsx
const cookieStore = await cookies()
const hasCookie = cookieStore.has('theme')
```
[4]

Reading works in Server Components because the browser sends cookie data to the server in the HTTP request headers. Setting or deleting cookies is not supported during Server Component rendering. For that, use a Server Function or a Route Handler. [3]

Create a Route Handler with the `route.ts` (or `route.js`) file convention inside the `app/` folder. Route Handlers are public HTTP endpoints, and a file at `/app/api/route.ts` handles requests to `/api` [4].

## Return JSON with `Response.json`

Export a function named after the HTTP method. Route Handlers use the Web `Request` and `Response` APIs [2].

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}
```
[2]

## Or use `NextResponse.json()`

`NextResponse.json()` produces a response with the given JSON body, and you can pass options such as a status code [1].

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' }, { status: 500 })
}
```
[1]

## Handle errors

Wrap operations that may throw in `try/catch` [4]:

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { submit } from '@/lib/submit'

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    await submit(request)
    return new Response(null, { status: 204 })
  } catch (reason) {
    const message =
      reason instanceof Error ? reason.message : 'Unexpected error'

    return new Response(message, { status: 500 })
  }
}
```
[4]

## Things to keep in mind

- Avoid exposing sensitive information in error messages sent to the client [4].
- Any client can reach Route Handlers, so implement authentication and authorization to restrict access [4].
- `create-next-app` with the `--api` flag includes an example `route.ts` in a new project [3].

The docs cover this for Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

**Route-segment level:** add an `error.tsx` file inside a route segment. It wraps that segment and its nested children in an error boundary and shows your fallback UI if something throws. It must be a Client Component. [2][4]

```tsx
'use client' // Error boundaries must be Client Components

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function ErrorPage({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: unknown
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[2][4]

Errors bubble up to the nearest parent error boundary, so you can place `error.tsx` files at different levels of the route hierarchy. [2] `error.js` does not wrap the `layout.js` or `template.js` above it in the same segment. For root layout errors, use `global-error.js`. [4]

**Component level:** for boundaries not tied to route segments, use `catchError`:

```tsx
'use client'

import { catchError, type ErrorInfo } from 'next/error'

function ErrorFallback(props: { title: string }, { error, retry }: ErrorInfo) {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <p>{message}</p>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default catchError(ErrorFallback)
```
[2]

Then wrap any part of your UI with the returned component:

```tsx
import ErrorBoundary from './custom-error-boundary'

export default function Component({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <ErrorBoundary title="Dashboard Error">{children}</ErrorBoundary>
}
```
[1]

**Caveat:** error boundaries don't catch errors in event handlers or async code. Catch those manually and store them with `useState` or `useReducer`, then render a fallback. [1] Unhandled errors inside `startTransition` do bubble up to the nearest error boundary. [1]

## Angular

Wrap part of your template in `@boundary`. If a component or directive inside throws during initialization or change detection, Angular renders the `@error` block instead:

```angular-html
@boundary {
  <app-risky-component />
} @error {
  <p>Something went wrong!</p>
}
```
[5]

The docs don't name a single "best" way, and the answer depends on your framework and deployment. The context covers Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

Use the `next/image` component. It optimizes images on demand, serves modern formats like WebP, and handles lazy loading and responsive sizing [5]. When self-hosting with `next start`, it works with zero configuration [3].

```tsx
import Image from 'next/image'

export default function Page() {
  return <Image alt="turtles" src="/turtles.jpg" width={300} height={300} />
}
```

If you use a static export (`output: 'export'`) or prefer a cloud provider such as Cloudinary, define a custom loader in `next.config.js` [1][4]:

```js
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  output: 'export',
  images: {
    loader: 'custom',
    loaderFile: './my-loader.ts',
  },
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

The loader file must export a default function that returns the image URL string [4]:

```ts
export default function cloudinaryLoader({
  src,
  width,
  quality,
}: {
  src: string
  width: number
  quality?: number
}) {
  const params = ['f_auto', 'c_limit', `w_${width}`, `q_${quality || 'auto'}`]
  return `https://res.cloudinary.com/demo/image/upload/${params.join(
    ','
  )}${src}`
}
```

If you already optimize images yourself, you can disable Image Optimization and keep the other benefits of `next/image` [3].

## Angular

The docs provided only mention that Angular's built-in image directive, `NgOptimizedImage`, generates `srcset`s automatically. They suggest it as a fix for oversized images [2]. They don't cover its setup or usage.

