Put it in a `proxy.ts` (or `.js`) file in the project root, or inside `src` if you use it, at the same level as `pages` or `app`. [3] Proxy runs on the server before a request is completed, and it executes before routes are rendered. [3]

If you've customized `pageExtensions` (for example to `.page.ts`), name the file `proxy.page.ts` or `proxy.page.js` instead. [3]

Note that the `middleware` file convention is deprecated and has been renamed to `proxy`. [3]

## Example: redirect unauthenticated users

```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { authenticate } from 'auth-provider'

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const isAuthenticated = authenticate(request)

  // If the user is authenticated, continue as normal
  if (isAuthenticated) {
    return NextResponse.next()
  }

  // Redirect to login page if not authenticated
  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/dashboard/:path*',
}
```
[1]

## Things to keep in mind for many routes

- **Use matchers.** Proxy is invoked for every route in your project, so use `matcher` to target or exclude specific routes. [5] For example, `'/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|.*\\.png$).*)'` excludes API routes, static assets, images, and `.png` files. [2]
- **Keep checks optimistic.** Since Proxy also runs on prefetched routes, only read the session from the cookie and avoid database checks to prevent performance issues. [2]
- **Execution order.** Proxy runs after `headers` and `redirects` in `next.config.js` and before `beforeFiles` rewrites and filesystem routes. [5]
- **Don't rely on Proxy alone for auth.** Server Functions are handled as POST requests to the route where they're used, so a matcher that excludes a path also skips Server Function calls there. Verify authentication and authorization inside each Server Function. [5]
- **Don't share modules or globals.** Proxy is meant to be invoked separately from your render code, so don't rely on shared modules or globals. To pass information to your app, use headers, cookies, rewrites, redirects, or the URL. [3]

Create a mock with `jest.fn()`, run the code under test, then assert on the arguments using matchers or the mock's `mock` property.

## Matchers

**Any call:** `.toHaveBeenCalledWith` checks that the mock was called with specific arguments, using the same algorithm as `.toEqual`. [3]

```js
test('registration applies correctly to orange La Croix', () => {
  const beverage = new LaCroix('orange');
  register(beverage);
  const f = jest.fn();
  applyToAll(f);
  expect(f).toHaveBeenCalledWith(beverage);
});
```
[3]

**A specific call:** `.toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(nthCall, ...args)` checks the arguments of the nth call. The count starts at 1. [4]

```js
test('drinkEach drinks each drink', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  drinkEach(drink, ['lemon', 'octopus']);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(1, 'lemon');
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(2, 'octopus');
});
```
[4]

**The last call:** `.toHaveBeenLastCalledWith` checks the arguments of the most recent call. [5]

```js
test('applying to all flavors does mango last', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  applyToAllFlavors(drink);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenLastCalledWith('mango');
});
```
[5]

## Inspecting arguments directly

- `mockFn.mock.calls` is an array with one entry per call, and each entry is the array of arguments for that call. For calls `f('arg1', 'arg2')` and `f('arg3', 'arg4')`, it is `[['arg1', 'arg2'], ['arg3', 'arg4']]`. [1]
- `mockFn.mock.lastCall` is the arguments of the last call, e.g. `['arg3', 'arg4']`. It is `undefined` if the mock was never called. [2]

Call `jest.mock('axios')` to automatically mock the whole module, then use `mockResolvedValue` on the methods you need to return fake data [1].

```js
import axios from 'axios';
import Users from './users';

jest.mock('axios');

test('should fetch users', () => {
  const users = [{name: 'Bob'}];
  const resp = {data: users};
  axios.get.mockResolvedValue(resp);

  // or you could use the following depending on your use case:
  // axios.get.mockImplementation(() => Promise.resolve(resp))

  return Users.all().then(data => expect(data).toEqual(users));
});
```
[1]

This way `axios.get('/users.json')` returns a fake response, so the test doesn't hit the real API [1].

Things to keep in mind:

- A module mocked with `jest.mock` is mocked only in the file that calls `jest.mock`. Other files importing it get the original implementation [2].
- Importing a module in a setup file (via `setupFilesAfterEnv`) prevents mocking of that module and everything it imports [2].
- To mock the same module differently within one file, use `jest.doMock()` with `jest.resetModules()` in `beforeEach`, and load the module with `require` [3]. With `babel-jest`, `jest.mock` calls are hoisted to the top of the block, and `doMock` avoids that [3].
- In TypeScript, use `jest.Mocked` or `jest.mocked()` to get typed mocks [2].

You have a few options in Jest. In every case, Jest has to be told to wait for the promise.

## Return the promise

Return a promise from your test and Jest waits for it to resolve. If the promise is rejected, the test fails. [2]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', () => {
  return fetchData().then(data => {
    expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
  });
});
```
[2]

## async/await

Put `async` in front of the test function and `await` the call. [3]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
});
```
[3]

## `.resolves` / `.rejects`

You can combine `async`/`await` with `.resolves` or `.rejects`. [3]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).resolves.toBe('peanut butter');
});

test('the fetch fails with an error', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).rejects.toMatch('error');
});
```
[3]

Without `async`/`await`, return the unwrapped assertion instead: [4]

```js
test('resolves to lemon', () => {
  return expect(Promise.resolve('lemon')).resolves.toBe('lemon');
});
```
[4]

## Testing rejections

If you expect a promise to be rejected, use `.catch` and add `expect.assertions` so a fulfilled promise doesn't pass silently. [3]

```js
test('the fetch fails with an error', () => {
  expect.assertions(1);
  return fetchData().catch(error => expect(error).toMatch('error'));
});
```
[3]

## Pitfalls

- Always `return` or `await` the promise. If you omit it, the test completes before the promise resolves or rejects. [3]
- Use `expect.assertions(n)` to verify that a certain number of assertions were called. [3]

Use `beforeEach`. It runs a function before each test in the file, which makes it a good place to reset global state. [1]

```js
beforeEach(() => {
  initializeCityDatabase();
});

afterEach(() => {
  clearCityDatabase();
});
```
[2]

Some details:

- **Async setup:** If the function returns a promise (or is a generator), Jest waits for it to resolve before running the test. [1] You can also use a `done` parameter. [2]

  ```js
  beforeEach(() => {
    return initializeCityDatabase();
  });
  ```
  [2]

- **Timeout:** You can pass a timeout in milliseconds as a second argument. The default is 5 seconds. [1]
- **Scope:** Inside a `describe` block, `beforeEach` runs only for the tests in that block. [1]
- **One-time setup:** If you only need setup to run once before any tests, use `beforeAll` instead. [1]

**Resetting modules or mocks:** If the state you need to reset is module state or mock state, there are dedicated options:

- Call `jest.resetModules()` inside `beforeEach` to reset the module registry, so each test gets a fresh copy of required modules. [3]
- Set the `resetModules` config option to `true` to do this automatically before each test. [4]
- Set the `resetMocks` config option to `true` to reset mock state before every test. This is equivalent to calling `jest.resetAllMocks()` before each test. It removes fake implementations but doesn't restore the initial ones. [5]

Yes. The docs describe two ways to run TypeScript tests and two ways to get types for Jest's globals.

## Running TypeScript tests

**Babel:** Install `@babel/preset-typescript` and add it to the presets in `babel.config.js` (after following the Babel setup) [3].

```javascript
module.exports = {
  presets: [
    ['@babel/preset-env', {targets: {node: 'current'}}],
    '@babel/preset-typescript',
  ],
};
```

Babel only transpiles, so Jest won't type-check your tests. For type-checking, use `ts-jest` or run `tsc` separately (or as part of your build) [3].

**ts-jest:** Install `ts-jest`. You may also need to create a configuration file for it [3].

```bash
npm install --save-dev ts-jest
```

## Typing the Jest globals

**`@jest/globals`:** Types ship with Jest and update whenever you update Jest. Install the package and import the APIs from it [3]:

```ts
import {describe, expect, test} from '@jest/globals';
import {sum} from './sum';

describe('sum module', () => {
  test('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {
    expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
  });
});
```

The TypeScript examples in the Globals docs only work as documented if you explicitly import the Jest APIs this way [2][4].

**`@types/jest`:** This package provides types for the globals without imports [1].

```bash
npm install --save-dev @types/jest
```

It is a third-party package maintained at DefinitelyTyped, so it may not cover the latest Jest features. Match its version to your Jest version as closely as possible (for Jest `27.4.0`, use `@types/jest` `27.4.x`) [1].

## Config files

If you write your Jest config in TypeScript (`jest.config.ts`), Jest requires `ts-node` by default. You can switch to `esbuild-register` with a `@jest-config-loader` docblock [5].

Use `jest.spyOn(object, methodName)`. It tracks calls to `object[methodName]` and, by default, still calls the original method, so the behavior isn't replaced. [1]

```js
const video = require('./video');

afterEach(() => {
  // restore the spy created with spyOn
  jest.restoreAllMocks();
});

test('plays video', () => {
  const spy = jest.spyOn(video, 'play');
  const isPlaying = video.play();

  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalled();
  expect(isPlaying).toBe(true);
});
```
[1]

Here the real `play()` still runs and returns `true`, while the spy records the call. [1]

- **Restoring:** Call `jest.restoreAllMocks()` in an `afterEach` hook to restore the original state. [1]
- **Getters and setters:** Pass `'get'` or `'set'` as a third argument, e.g. `jest.spyOn(video, 'play', 'get')`. [2]
- **Automatic restore:** If your setup transpiles explicit resource management (e.g. TypeScript >= 5.2), you can write `using spy = jest.spyOn(console, 'warn');` and the spy is restored when the block exits. [1][4]

If you later want to override the behavior instead, add `.mockImplementation(() => customImplementation)`. [1]

Jest can mock imported ES6 classes because a class is just a constructor function, so the mock must be a function or another ES6 class. You can build it with mock functions [1].

## Option 1: Module factory with `jest.mock()`

Pass a module factory (a function that returns the mock). To mock a constructor, the factory must return a constructor function [4]:

```javascript
import SoundPlayer from './sound-player';
const mockPlaySoundFile = jest.fn();
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
    return {playSoundFile: mockPlaySoundFile};
  });
});
```
[4]

Because `jest.mock()` calls are hoisted, Jest blocks access to out-of-scope variables in the factory. The check is disabled for variables whose names start with `mock`, but you must make sure they're initialized in time (watch for the Temporal Dead Zone) [4]. For example, using `fakePlaySoundFile` fails, and defining `mockSoundPlayer` outside the factory and returning it directly causes a `ReferenceError` [4].

## Spying on the constructor

Using `jest.fn().mockImplementation()` lets you track constructor calls [3]:

```javascript
expect(SoundPlayer).toHaveBeenCalled();
// or
expect(SoundPlayer.mock.calls.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
```
[3]

## Non-default exports

If the class isn't the default export, return an object keyed by the export name [3]:

```javascript
import {SoundPlayer} from './sound-player';
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return {
    SoundPlayer: jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
      return {playSoundFile: () => {}};
    }),
  };
});
```
[3]

## Option 2: Manual mock in `__mocks__`

Create an ES6 class with the same filename in a `__mocks__` folder, and it will be used in place of the real class. This lets you inject a test implementation but gives you no way to spy on calls [5]:

```javascript
// __mocks__/sound-player.js
export default class SoundPlayer {
  constructor() {
    console.log('Mock SoundPlayer: constructor was called');
  }

  playSoundFile() {
    console.log('Mock SoundPlayer: playSoundFile was called');
  }
}
```
[5]

The provided docs list four ways to create an ES6 class mock, but only the module factory and manual mock approaches are covered here.

Use the `jsdom` test environment. It emulates a browser by providing browser APIs like `document` and `window`. The default is `node`. [2][5]

**1. Install the package** (needed for the JSDOM environment if it isn't installed already): [4]

```bash
npm install --save-dev jest-environment-jsdom
```

**2. Choose the environment.** The docs show a per-file option, which uses a `@jest-environment` docblock at the top of the file and applies to all tests in that file: [5]

```js
/**
 * @jest-environment jsdom
 */

test('use jsdom in this test file', () => {
  const element = document.createElement('div');
  expect(element).not.toBeNull();
});
```

The `testEnvironment` configuration option also controls the environment (default `node`), and you can use `jsdom` there for a browser-like environment. [5] The provided docs don't include a config-file example for setting it globally.

**Optional: customize jsdom.** Use `testEnvironmentOptions`, which can also be passed in a docblock as `JSON.parse`-able text. For example, to set the URL: [3]

```js
/**
 * @jest-environment jsdom
 * @jest-environment-options {"url": "https://jestjs.io/"}
 */

test('use jsdom and set the URL in this test file', () => {
  expect(window.location.href).toBe('https://jestjs.io/');
});
```

## Setup

**1. Install the testing library.** `@testing-library/react-native` replaces the deprecated `react-test-renderer`, and needs `test-renderer` as a peer dependency [1]:

```bash
yarn add --dev @testing-library/react-native test-renderer
```

**2. Write the test.** Render the component and pass its JSON output to `toMatchSnapshot()` [1]:

```tsx
import {render, screen} from '@testing-library/react-native';
import React from 'react';
import Intro from '../Intro';

test('renders correctly', async () => {
  await render(<Intro />);
  expect(screen.toJSON()).toMatchSnapshot();
});
```

The docs place this at `__tests__/Intro-test.js` [1].

**3. Run it.** Run `yarn test` or `jest`. The first run writes a snapshot file, e.g. `__tests__/__snapshots__/Intro-test.js.snap` [2].

## Workflow

- On later runs, Jest compares the rendered output to the stored snapshot [2].
- Commit the snapshot along with code changes [2].
- If a snapshot test fails, check whether the change is intended or not. If it is expected, run `jest -u` to overwrite the snapshot [2].

## Caveats

The React Native docs note that snapshots can be hard to review when large, that a snapshot is treated as correct when created even if the output is wrong, and that it's tempting to update failing snapshots without investigating [4].

The provided docs don't cover any Jest configuration (such as presets) for React Native projects.

In React Native, use the `BackHandler` API (Android-only). It lets you register listeners for the hardware back press and control how your app responds. [3]

## Register a listener

`BackHandler.addEventListener` creates a listener and returns an `EventSubscription`, which you should clear with `remove`. Return `true` from the handler to stop the default behavior:

```jsx
import {useEffect} from 'react';
import {BackHandler, Alert} from 'react-native';

useEffect(() => {
  const backAction = () => {
    Alert.alert('Hold on!', 'Are you sure you want to go back?', [
      {text: 'Cancel', onPress: () => null, style: 'cancel'},
      {text: 'YES', onPress: () => BackHandler.exitApp()},
    ]);
    return true;
  };

  const backHandler = BackHandler.addEventListener(
    'hardwareBackPress',
    backAction,
  );

  return () => backHandler.remove();
}, []);
```
[5]

## How handlers are called

- Subscriptions are called in reverse order of registration, so the last one registered runs first. [3]
- If one returns `true`, earlier subscriptions are not called. [3]
- If none return `true` (or none are registered), the default back behavior runs and exits the app. [3]
- If a `Modal` is open, `BackHandler` publishes no events. [3]

## Other cases

- **React Navigation:** follow their guide on [Custom Android back button behaviour](https://reactnavigation.org/docs/custom-android-back-button-handling/). [2]
- **Host Activity that isn't a `ReactActivity`** (Android Fragment integration): implement `DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler` and delegate `invokeDefaultOnBackPressed()` to the `OnBackPressedDispatcher`. [4]

```kotlin
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity(), DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler {
    override fun invokeDefaultOnBackPressed() {
        onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed()
    }
}
```
[4]

React Native gives you two ways to separate code by platform [3].

## 1. The `Platform` module

Use this when only small parts of a component are platform-specific [1].

`Platform.OS` is `ios` on iOS and `android` on Android [1]:

```tsx
import {Platform, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  height: Platform.OS === 'ios' ? 200 : 100,
});
```

`Platform.select` takes an object with keys `'ios' | 'android' | 'native' | 'default'` and returns the best match for the current platform. On a phone, `ios` and `android` take preference, then `native`, then `default` [1].

```tsx
import {Platform, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    ...Platform.select({
      ios: {
        backgroundColor: 'red',
      },
      android: {
        backgroundColor: 'green',
      },
      default: {
        // other platforms, web for example
        backgroundColor: 'blue',
      },
    }),
  },
});
```

Because `Platform.select` accepts any value, it can also return platform-specific components [1]:

```tsx
const Component = Platform.select({
  ios: () => require('ComponentIOS'),
  android: () => require('ComponentAndroid'),
})();

<Component />;
```

On Android, you can also check the API version with `Platform.Version`. This is the Android API version, not the OS version [4]:

```tsx
import {Platform} from 'react-native';

if (Platform.Version === 25) {
  console.log('Running on Nougat!');
}
```

## 2. Platform-specific file extensions

For more complex differences, split the code into separate files. React Native detects `.ios.` and `.android.` extensions and loads the right file [2].

```shell
BigButton.ios.js
BigButton.android.js
```

Then import without the platform extension [2]:

```tsx
import BigButton from './BigButton';
```

The docs cover this for both Angular and React Native, so the answer depends on which you're using.

## Angular

`HttpClient` has a method for each HTTP verb. Each returns an RxJS `Observable` that sends the request when subscribed and emits the result when the server responds. [2] For a GET request, pass the endpoint URL and an optional options object:

```ts
http.get<Config>('/api/config').subscribe((config) => {
  // process the configuration.
});
```
[3]

- The generic type (`Config`) is optional. If you omit it, the data is typed as `Object`. [3]
- The generic is only a type assertion, and `HttpClient` doesn't verify that the server's response matches it. [3]
- Each subscription to the Observable makes a new backend request. [2]

The docs provided only list "Setting up HttpClient" as a heading [1], so they don't explain how to configure or inject it.

## React Native

Use the built-in Fetch API. Pass the URL to `fetch`:

```ts
fetch('https://mywebsite.com/mydata.json');
```
[4]

For a POST request or custom headers, pass an options object as the second argument:

```ts
fetch('https://mywebsite.com/endpoint/', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    firstParam: 'yourValue',
    secondParam: 'yourOtherValue',
  }),
});
```
[4]

Use the `useWindowDimensions` hook. It automatically updates its values when the screen size or font scale changes, so your component re-renders on rotation. [1]

```tsx
import {useWindowDimensions} from 'react-native';

const {height, width} = useWindowDimensions();
```
[1]

`width` is the width in pixels of the window or screen your app occupies. [3] The docs call this the preferred API for React components because, unlike `Dimensions`, it updates as the window's dimensions update. [2]

Use the values inline in your render logic, as in the docs' example, which sizes a `View` from the hook's values in an inline `style`. [4]

## Alternative: `Dimensions`

If you use `Dimensions.get('window')`, the values can change (for example through rotation or on foldable devices). Read them on every render rather than caching them, for example by using inline styles instead of setting a value in a `StyleSheet`. [2] To react to changes, subscribe with the event listener: [2][5]

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = Dimensions.addEventListener(
    'change',
    ({window, screen}: DimensionsPayload) => {
      if (window && screen) {
        setDimensions({window, screen});
      }
    },
  );
  return () => subscription?.remove();
});
```
[5]

Use `Alert.alert` from `react-native`, passing a title, a message, and an array of buttons. Each button's `onPress` callback fires when it is tapped, and tapping any button dismisses the alert [3]. Setting `style: 'cancel'` on the Cancel button marks it as the cancel button:

```tsx
import {Button, Alert} from 'react-native';

const createTwoButtonAlert = () =>
  Alert.alert('Alert Title', 'My Alert Msg', [
    {
      text: 'Cancel',
      onPress: () => console.log('Cancel Pressed'),
      style: 'cancel',
    },
    {text: 'OK', onPress: () => console.log('OK Pressed')},
  ]);

<Button title={'2-Button Alert'} onPress={createTwoButtonAlert} />
```
[1]

**Android notes:**
- With two buttons, Android treats them as 'negative' and 'positive' (such as 'Cancel', 'OK'). [2]
- By default, tapping outside the alert does not dismiss it. To allow that, pass `{cancelable: true}` as a fourth argument, and optionally add an `onDismiss` callback to handle the dismissal [2][5].

```tsx
Alert.alert('Alert Title', 'My Alert Msg', [{text: 'Cancel', style: 'cancel'}], {
  cancelable: true,
  onDismiss: () => console.log('Dismissed'),
});
```
[2]

`Alert` works on both Android and iOS for static alerts [3].

Use `Linking.openURL()` from React Native. It tries to open the given URL with any installed app, and for `http`/`https` URLs that is the device's browser [1][2].

```tsx
import {Linking} from 'react-native';

await Linking.openURL('https://google.com');
```

Things to keep in mind:

- **Include the protocol.** For web URLs, the `"http://"` or `"https://"` prefix must be set [1].
- **It returns a Promise.** The promise resolves if the user confirms the open dialog or the URL opens automatically. It rejects if the user cancels or no registered app can handle the URL [1].
- **Check first for non-http(s) URLs.** If you pass a non-http(s) URL, it's best to call `canOpenURL()` first [1].

Here is the docs' example, which checks support before opening and shows an alert otherwise [5]:

```tsx
import {useCallback} from 'react';
import {Alert, Button, Linking} from 'react-native';

type OpenURLButtonProps = {
  url: string;
  children: string;
};

const OpenURLButton = ({url, children}: OpenURLButtonProps) => {
  const handlePress = useCallback(async () => {
    const supported = await Linking.canOpenURL(url);

    if (supported) {
      await Linking.openURL(url);
    } else {
      Alert.alert(`Don't know how to open this URL: ${url}`);
    }
  }, [url]);

  return <Button title={children} onPress={handlePress} />;
};
```

On Android, `canOpenURL()` needs a `<queries>` entry in your manifest for the scheme. For `https`, add [3]:

```xml
<manifest ...>
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
      <data android:scheme="https"/>
    </intent>
  </queries>
</manifest>
```

Use `Pressable` and pass a function to its `style` prop. The function receives a boolean `pressed` and returns view styles. [1]

```tsx
<Pressable
  onPress={() => {
    setTimesPressed(current => current + 1);
  }}
  style={({pressed}) => [
    {
      backgroundColor: pressed ? 'rgb(210, 230, 255)' : 'white',
    },
    styles.wrapperCustom,
  ]}>
  {({pressed}) => (
    <Text style={styles.text}>{pressed ? 'Pressed!' : 'Press Me'}</Text>
