`children` can also be a function that receives `pressed`, so you can change the content (such as the text) while the button is pressed. [5]

The built-in `Button` component only supports a minimal level of customization. If it doesn't fit your app, the docs recommend building your own button with `Pressable`. [3]

In React Native, use the `Animated` API: bind a view's `opacity` to an `Animated.Value` and animate it with `Animated.timing`.

```tsx
import {useEffect, useRef} from 'react';
import {Animated, Text, View} from 'react-native';

const FadeInView = props => {
  const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current; // Initial value for opacity: 0

  useEffect(() => {
    Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
      toValue: 1,
      duration: 10000,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [fadeAnim]);

  return (
    <Animated.View // Special animatable View
      style={{
        ...props.style,
        opacity: fadeAnim, // Bind opacity to animated value
      }}>
      {props.children}
    </Animated.View>
  );
};
```
[3]

How it works:

- `fadeAnim` is created with `useRef` and starts at 0, so the opacity is 0 when the component mounts. The `opacity` of the `Animated.View` is mapped to this value. [1]
- `Animated.timing` then animates `fadeAnim` to 1, updating the dependent opacity on each frame. [1]
- You can then use `FadeInView` in place of a `View`. [1][3]

You can also use the `useAnimatedValue` hook instead of `useRef(new Animated.Value(0))`, and trigger the fade from a function such as a button press, rather than on mount:

```tsx
const fadeAnim = useAnimatedValue(0);

const fadeIn = () => {
  Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
    toValue: 1,
    duration: 5000,
    useNativeDriver: true,
  }).start();
};
```
[2]

`Animated` exports animatable versions of `View`, `Text`, `Image`, `ScrollView`, `FlatList` and `SectionList`. For other components, use `Animated.createAnimatedComponent()`. [3]

**Angular:** the docs provided only cover animating an element entering a view, with `animate.enter` applying a CSS class that can fade in via `opacity` keyframes. [5] They don't cover the general case of fading a view in. If you meant Angular, say so and I can go from that example.

```html
@if (isShown()) {
  <div class="insert-container" animate.enter="enter-animation">
    <p>The box is inserted</p>
  </div>
}
```
[5]

```css
.enter-animation {
  animation: slide-fade 1s;
}

@keyframes slide-fade {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}
```
[5]

Use `toSignal` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`. It creates a signal that tracks the value of an Observable, similar to the `async` pipe but usable anywhere in an application [3].

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {interval} from 'rxjs';
import {toSignal} from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

@Component({
  template: `{{ counter() }}`,
})
export class Ticker {
  counterObservable = interval(1000);

  // Get a `Signal` representing the `counterObservable`'s value.
  counter = toSignal(this.counterObservable, {initialValue: 0});
}
```
[3]

**Key points**

- **Subscription:** `toSignal` subscribes immediately, which may trigger side effects. It unsubscribes automatically when the component or service that called it is destroyed. Avoid calling it repeatedly for the same Observable; reuse the returned signal [3].
- **Injection context:** It must run in an injection context, such as during construction of a component or service. Otherwise, pass an `Injector` manually [3].
- **Initial values:** Signals always need a current value, so you have three options [4]:
  - `initialValue` sets the value returned before the Observable first emits.
  - With no `initialValue`, the signal returns `undefined` until the first emission.
  - `requireSync: true` is for Observables guaranteed to emit synchronously, like `BehaviorSubject`. The signal then always has a value, with no `undefined` type.
- **Errors and completion:** If the Observable errors, the error is thrown when the signal is read. If it completes, the signal keeps returning the last emitted value [1].
- **Cleanup:** Pass `manualCleanup` to override automatic unsubscription, which is useful for Observables that complete on their own [5].
- **Custom equality:** The `equal` option lets you define when consecutive values are considered the same. Equal values don't update the signal [5].

Use the `loadComponent` property on the route (this is the Angular router). It lazily loads the component's JavaScript when the route becomes active. [1]

```ts
import {Routes} from '@angular/router';

export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'login',
    loadComponent: () => import('./components/auth/login-page'),
  },
];
```
[1]

Key points:

- `loadComponent` takes a loader function that returns a Promise resolving to an Angular component. Usually this is the standard dynamic `import()`, but any async loader function works. [1]
- If the file uses a `default` export, return the `import()` promise directly, with no extra `.then` to pick the exported class. [1]
- To lazily load child routes, use `loadChildren`, which loads them during route matching. [1]
- Lazy loading puts the component in a separate JavaScript chunk, which the router requests only when the user visits that route. This removes code from the initial bundle and can speed up load. [1]
- Loader functions run in the route's injection context, so you can call `inject()` inside them, for example to choose which component to import based on a feature flag. [3]

```ts
loadComponent: () => {
  const flags = inject(FeatureFlags);
  return flags.isPremium
    ? import('./dashboard/premium-dashboard')
    : import('./dashboard/basic-dashboard');
},
```
[3]

By contrast, using the `component` property eagerly bundles the component with the route config. [2] The docs generally recommend eager loading for primary landing pages and lazy loading for the rest. [5]

The question doesn't name a framework, so here is what the docs provided show for Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

**Option 1: `unauthorized()` in a page.** Verify the session and call `unauthorized()` when there isn't one. This renders your `unauthorized.tsx` file, which can show a login UI.

```tsx
import { verifySession } from '@/app/lib/dal'
import { unauthorized } from 'next/navigation'

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const session = await verifySession()

  if (!session) {
    unauthorized()
  }

  return <div>Dashboard</div>
}
```
[5]

```tsx
import Login from '@/app/components/Login'

export default function UnauthorizedPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>401 - Unauthorized</h1>
      <p>Please log in to access this page.</p>
      <Login />
    </main>
  )
}
```
[5]

**Option 2: Route Handlers.** The same `unauthorized()` call works in an API route, so only authenticated users can reach the endpoint. It returns a 401. [1]

```tsx
export async function GET(req: NextRequest): Promise<NextResponse> {
  const session = await verifySession()

  if (!session) {
    unauthorized()
  }

  // Fetch data
  // ...
}
```
[1]

**Option 3: Proxy (optimistic redirects).** In `proxy.ts`, check whether the route is protected, read the session from the cookie, and redirect unauthenticated users to `/login`. [4]

```tsx
if (isProtectedRoute && !session?.userId) {
  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', req.nextUrl))
}
```
[4]

Because Proxy runs on every route, including prefetched ones, only read the session from the cookie there and avoid database checks. [4]

## Angular

Use a route guard (`CanActivateFn`) that returns `true` when the user is authenticated and otherwise returns a `UrlTree` pointing to `/login`:

```ts
export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  const authStore = inject(AuthStore);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return authStore.isAuthenticated() ? true : router.parseUrl('/login');
};
```
[2]

The docs provided don't show here how to attach the guard to a route definition.

Use an HTTP interceptor that injects your `AuthService`, clones the request, and adds the token header. Interceptors run in the injection context of the injector that registered them, so they can call `inject` to get dependencies [2].

```ts
export function authInterceptor(req: HttpRequest<unknown>, next: HttpHandlerFn) {
  // Inject the current `AuthService` and use it to get an authentication token:
  const authToken = inject(AuthService).getAuthToken();

  // Clone the request to add the authentication header.
  const newReq = req.clone({
    headers: req.headers.append('X-Authentication-Token', authToken),
  });
  return next(newReq);
}
```
[2]

A class-based version is also possible, using `@Injectable()` and `HttpInterceptor`:

```ts
@Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  private authService = inject(AuthService);

  intercept(request: HttpRequest<unknown>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<unknown>> {
    const clonedRequest = request.clone({
      headers: request.headers.append('X-Authentication-Token', this.authService.getAuthToken()),
    });
    return next.handle(clonedRequest);
  }
}
```
[1]

**Testing it:** configure `TestBed` with `withInterceptors` for the functional interceptor. The docs recommend testing one interceptor at a time [1].

```ts
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [
    AuthService,
    // Testing one interceptor at a time is recommended.
    provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor])),
    provideHttpClientTesting(),
  ],
});
```
[1]

**Not covered:** the provided docs don't show how to register the interceptor in your application config for production (the context only shows the `TestBed` setup with `withInterceptors`). They also don't show the `TestBed` configuration for the class-based interceptor.

**SSR caveat:** with HttpClient transfer caching, requests with `Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, or `Cookie` headers are excluded from caching by default, to avoid caching user-specific responses [4].

Add `provideHttpClient` to the `providers` array in `app.config.ts`. [2]

```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(/* add features here, such as withInterceptors(...) */)],
};
```
[2]

Then inject `HttpClient` into your components, services, or other classes: [2]

```ts
@Service()
export class ConfigService {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  // This service can now make HTTP requests via `this.http`.
}
```
[2]

**Notes**

- In Angular v21 and later, `HttpClient` is available for injection by default. [1] The docs provided don't say whether `provideHttpClient` is still needed in that case, for example to add features.
- `provideHttpClient` accepts optional features such as `withInterceptors(...)` and `withXhr()`. [4]
- By default, `HttpClient` uses the `fetch` API. `withXhr()` switches it to `XMLHttpRequest`, but you shouldn't use it in SSR environments. [4]

Use the `@for` block. It loops over any JavaScript iterable (with extra optimizations for arrays) and renders its content once per item. A `track` expression is required:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id) {
  {{ item.name }}
}
```
[1]

## Choosing a `track` expression

`track` lets Angular map data to DOM nodes so it does the minimum DOM work when data changes [1].

- **Unique property:** Use something like `id` or `uuid` when your data has one [1].
- **`$index`:** Fine for static collections that never change [1].
- **The item itself (`track item`):** Tracks by reference identity (`===`). Use it only as a last resort, since it can make rendering updates significantly slower [1].

## Contextual variables

Inside the block you get `$count`, `$index`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even`, and `$odd`. You can alias them with `let`:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id; let idx = $index, e = $even) {
  <p>Item #{{ idx }}: {{ item.name }}</p>
}
```
[2]

## Fallback for empty lists

Add an `@empty` block right after the `@for` content:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.name) {
  <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>There are no items.</li>
}
```
[3]

## Limitations

`@for` doesn't support `continue` or `break` [1].

Call `input.required` instead of `input` when declaring the input:

```ts
@Component(/* ... */)
export class CustomSlider {
  // Declare a required input named value. Returns an `InputSignal<number>`.
  value = input.required<number>();
}
```
[1]

Angular enforces that required inputs must be set wherever the component is used in a template. If you omit any required input, Angular reports a build-time error. [1]

Notes:
- Required inputs do not automatically include `undefined` in the generic parameter of the returned `InputSignal`. [1]
- Reading a required input before its value is set causes an error (NG0950). To avoid it, access the input in reactive contexts such as the template, a `computed`, or an effect, or read it in `ngOnInit` or later. [3]

In Angular, a child component sends an event to its parent through an output: it calls `emit` on the output, and the parent listens for it in its template.

**Emit from the child.** You can emit primitive values or custom event objects:

```ts
// You can emit primitive values.
this.valueChanged.emit(7);

// You can emit custom event objects
this.thumbDropped.emit({
  pointerX: 123,
  pointerY: 456,
});
```
[1]

**Listen in the parent's template.** The event data is available as `$event`:

```angular-html
<custom-slider (valueChanged)="logValue($event)" />
```
[1]

**Receive the data in the parent component:**

```ts
@Component({
 /*...*/
})
export class App {
  logValue(value: number) {
    ...
  }
}
```
[1]

In tests, the output is an `OutputEmitterRef`, so you can call `subscribe` on it to detect what the child emitted. This is what the host component does implicitly [5].

The docs provided don't show how to declare the output property itself (for example, the `output()` function or `@Output`). They also don't cover child-to-parent communication in React Native or Next.js beyond Angular's outputs. The Next.js example here avoids callbacks by exposing state as a CSS attribute (`data-pending`) that ancestors react to [4].

Provide it at the root level. Services are provisioned at the root by default, which gives you a single shared instance for the whole application, global availability without manual provider registration, and tree-shakability. [2]

**Option 1: `@Service`**, a shorthand for `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })`:

```ts
import {Service} from '@angular/core';

@Service()
export class UserClient {
  // Single instance shared across all components
}
```
[1][2]

**Option 2: `@Injectable` with `providedIn: 'root'`.** This is the traditional form, and the root provider has to be set explicitly. [2]

**Option 3: Provide it in `bootstrapApplication`** for a true singleton, which suits global configuration, logging, or auth services. This is also the right choice if the provider has side effects or needs configuration. [4]

```ts
bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    LoggingService, // Used throughout the app
  ],
});
```
[4]

Things to note:

- `@Service` doesn't support constructor-based DI, advanced provider keys like `useClass`, or non-root scopes like `platform`. Use `@Injectable` if you need those. [2]
- Don't add the service to a component's `providers` array. That creates a new instance for each component instance, so you lose the singleton. [1]
- Providers registered in `bootstrapApplication` are always included in your bundle, even if never injected. [4]

Add the built-in `Validators.required` validator as the second item in the control's array. First import `Validators` from `@angular/forms` [2]:

```ts
import {Validators} from '@angular/forms';
```

Then use it on the control:

```ts
private formBuilder = inject(FormBuilder);

profileForm = this.formBuilder.group({
  firstName: ['', Validators.required],
  lastName: [''],
  address: this.formBuilder.group({
    street: [''],
    city: [''],
    state: [''],
    zip: [''],
  }),
});
```
[2]

A required control starts out invalid, and that status propagates to the parent form group. You can read it through the group's `status` property [2]:

```html
<p>Form Status: {{ profileForm.status }}</p>
```
[2]

To show an error message, check `hasError('required')` on the control in the template [3]:

```html
@if (name.invalid && (name.dirty || name.touched)) {
  @if (name.hasError('required')) {
    <div>Name is required.</div>
  }
}
```
[3]

Keep the `required` attribute in the template for accessibility. It isn't needed for validation itself [3].

The context covers this for Angular only. (Next.js is mentioned only in a glossary definition of hydration [4], with no enablement steps.)

**Prerequisite:** Hydration works only for SSR applications, so enable server-side rendering first [1].

### With Angular CLI

If you enabled SSR through the CLI (during app creation or later with `ng add @angular/ssr`), the code that enables hydration should already be in your app [1].

### Manual setup

For a custom setup, import `provideClientHydration` from `@angular/platform-browser` and add it to your bootstrapping providers [1].

Standalone:

```typescript
import {
  bootstrapApplication,
  provideClientHydration,
} from '@angular/platform-browser';
...

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration()]
});
```
[1]

NgModules, in the root app module's providers:

```typescript
import {provideClientHydration} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [App],
  exports: [App],
  bootstrap: [App],
  providers: [provideClientHydration()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```
[1]

### Server bootstrap

Make sure `provideClientHydration()` is also in the providers used to bootstrap the app on the **server**. With the default `ng new` project structure, adding it to the root `AppModule` is enough because the server module imports it. With a custom setup, add it to the providers list in the server bootstrap configuration [1][2].
